
## Overview

This document defines naming standards used throughout the data warehouse.

## General Rules

- Use lowercase letters.
- Use `snake_case`.
- Use English names.
- Avoid spaces.
- Avoid special characters.
- Avoid SQL reserved words.
- Keep names simple and descriptive.

## Table Naming

### Bronze Layer

Bronze tables use the original source system and entity name.

Format:

`<source>_<entity>`

Examples:

- `crm_cust_info`
- `crm_prd_info`
- `crm_sales_details`
- `erp_cust_az12`
- `erp_loc_a101`
- `erp_px_cat_g1v2`

### Silver Layer

Silver tables follow the same source-based naming convention.

Format:

`<source>_<entity>`

Examples:

- `crm_cust_info`
- `crm_prd_info`
- `crm_sales_details`

### Gold Layer

Gold tables use business-friendly names.

Dimension tables:

`dim_<entity>`

Fact tables:

`fact_<entity>`

Examples:

- `dim_customers`
- `dim_products`
- `fact_sales`

## Column Naming

### Surrogate Keys

Use the `_key` suffix.

Example:

`customer_key`

`product_key`

### Identifiers

Use descriptive names such as:

- `customer_id`
- `product_id`
- `order_number`

### Dates

Use descriptive names ending with `_date`.

Examples:

- `order_date`
- `shipping_date`
- `birthdate`
- `create_date`

## Stored Procedures

Loading procedures follow:

`load_<layer>`

Examples:

- `load_bronze`
- `load_silver`
- `load_gold`

## Summary

| Object | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Bronze table | `<source>_<entity>` | `crm_cust_info` |
| Silver table | `<source>_<entity>` | `crm_cust_info` |
| Dimension | `dim_<entity>` | `dim_customers` |
| Fact | `fact_<entity>` | `fact_sales` |
| Surrogate key | `<entity>_key` | `customer_key` |
| Date column | Descriptive `_date` | `order_date` |
| Procedure | `load_<layer>` | `load_bronze` |
